{
  "about": "Strip format 3 rows with the computed palette (docs/specs/232.md, part C, 'The computed palette'; section 6), as captured colours, written by hand. Each row is 1 pixel a square on line 1 of a capture 3 lines high. Lines 0 and 2, the 40 pixels before the row and the pixels after it are the game: `around`, repeated from the line's left edge. The row: `head` (squares 0 to 28 as two-level digits: the finder, four black level triples, the depth digit 1, the layout 0, the session 4711 as 0 1 1 1 4 7, the levels square L - 1), then `ramp` (3 x (L - 1) + 1 colours: code 0; red levels 1 to L - 1; green; blue), then `more` (two digits), then one square for each code of `codes`, then 4 black squares. A frame square of code c has red `paint[0][r]`, green `paint[1][g]`, blue `paint[2][b]`, where r, g and b are the code's levels. `codes` at 6 bits is the frame's bytes in base64 without '=' (a base64 digit is 6 bits: r its top two bits, g the next two, b the last two); at 9 bits it is the frame's bits in octal, three octal digits a square (r, g and b are the three digits). The last square is filled with 0 bits. `frame` is the frame of `tests/vectors/strip3.json` named 'fixed part only' (88 bytes); null when the row has no frame. `gap` and `leak` are worked out from `ramp` by part C, step 4; `failed` is part C, step 5.",
